The first value changes on every new row. After choosing that first value, the code flips the value after each print. This keeps the numbers alternating across the row.

A dry run for `n = 3` starts with `1` on row 1, then `0` on row 2, and then `1` on row 3.

```java
class Solution {
    public void pattern11(int n) {
        for (int row = 1; row <= n; row++) {
            int value = (row % 2 == 1) ? 1 : 0;

            for (int col = 1; col <= row; col++) {
                System.out.print(value);

                if (col < row) {
                    System.out.print(" ");
                }

                value = 1 - value;
            }

            System.out.println();
        }
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`. For `n <= 0`, the loops do not print anything.

The value at a position depends on the row and column together. If `(row + column)` is even, I print `1`; otherwise I print `0`. This gives the required alternating pattern without storing anything.

For `n = 3`, the rows are `1`, `0 1`, and `1 0 1`.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    void pattern11(int n) {
        for (int row = 1; row <= n; row++) {
            for (int col = 1; col <= row; col++) {
                int value = ((row + col) % 2 == 0) ? 1 : 0;
                cout << value;

                if (col < row) {
                    cout << " ";
                }
            }
            cout << "\n";
        }
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` because all positions in the triangle are printed.
Space complexity: `O(1)` apart from the output.

A bitwise check gives the same alternating result without calculating a separate start value. The lowest bit of `row + col` is `0` for an even number and `1` for an odd number, so `1 - ((row + col) & 1)` produces the needed pattern.

Dry run for row 2: column 1 gives `1`, and column 2 gives `0`, so the row is `1 0`.

```python
class Solution:
    def pattern11(self, n):
        for row in range(1, n + 1):
            for col in range(1, row + 1):
                value = 1 - ((row + col) & 1)
                print(value, end="" if col == row else " ")
            print()
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`. The input is not changed, and no extra row storage is used.

I used recursion here to print one row at a time. The helper prints the values in the current row, and then the main method moves to the next row. The starting value follows the row parity.

The empty or zero-size case is safe because the first call is skipped when `n` is not positive.

```cpp
class Solution {
    void printRow(int row, int col, int value) {
        if (col > row) {
            cout << "\n";
            return;
        }

        cout << value;
        if (col < row) {
            cout << " ";
        }

        printRow(row, col + 1, 1 - value);
    }

    void printRows(int row, int n) {
        if (row > n) {
            return;
        }

        int first = (row % 2 == 1) ? 1 : 0;
        printRow(row, 1, first);
        printRows(row + 1, n);
    }

public:
    void pattern11(int n) {
        if (n > 0) {
            printRows(1, n);
        }
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` for the printed cells.
Space complexity: `O(n)` because the row and column recursion can reach depth `O(n)`.

## Intuition
Tried a different approach using the `sum of row and column number` (position) for each element to determine whether to print 1/0.

## Approach
In this case, I considered both row (`i`) and column (`j`) starting from 0.
If the sum (`i+j`) is **even**, print `1`
Else, print `0`

## Solution
```java[]
class Solution {
    public void pattern11(int n) {
        for (int i=0; i<n; i++) {
            for (int j=0; j<=i; j++) {
                if ((i+j) % 2 == 0) {
                    System.out.print(1);
                }
                else {
                    System.out.print(0);
                }
                System.out.print(" ");
            }
            System.out.println();
        }
    }
}
```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity:** 
O(n^2)

**Space Complexity:**
O(1)

## Intuition
For each cell in the triangular pattern, the value depends on whether `(i+j)` is even or odd. Alternating this parity naturally produces a checkerboard-style triangle, so the pattern can be generated directly without extra state.

## Approach
Iterate `i` from `1` to `n` for rows, and `j` from `1` to `i` for columns in that row. At each position, print `1` if `(i+j)` is even, else print `0`. Move to a new line after each row.

## Solution
```cpp[]
class Solution {
public:
    void pattern11(int n) {
        int f=1;
        for(int i=1;i<=n;i++)
        {
            for(int j=1;j<=i;j++)
            {
                cout<<((i+j)%2==0?1:0)<<" ";
            }


            cout<<endl;
        }
    }
};
```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity:** O(n²), since the inner loop runs `i` times for each of the `n` outer iterations, giving a total of roughly n(n+1)/2 operations.

**Space Complexity:** O(1), as only a fixed number of integer variables are used regardless of input size.

## Solution
```python[]
class Solution:
    def pattern11(self, n):
        for i in range(n):
            for j in range(i+1):
                if (i+j) % 2== 0:
                    print(1,end='')
                    print(" ",end='')
                else:
                    print(0,end='')
                    print(" ",end='')
            print()
